\DOC ENSURES_WHILE_PUP_TAC

\TYPE {ENSURES_WHILE_PUP_TAC : term -> term -> term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Reduces a single {ensures} goal about a machine program containing a counted
loop to one conjunctive goal packaging a nonzero-trip-count side condition
together with four {ensures} subgoals (setup, loop body, back-edge, and exit)
expressed in terms of a supplied loop invariant.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes four terms: a {:num} trip count {t} bounding the loop
variable; two program-counter terms {pctop} and {pcbot} (given either as
explicit {pc}-offsets or as offset descriptors) naming the loop's top and
bottom code points; and an invariant of the form {\i s. ...} relating the loop
index to the machine state.
{
   A ?- ensures ...
   ========================================================  ENSURES_WHILE_PUP_TAC t pctop pcbot (\i s. ...)
   A ?- ~(t = 0) /\
        (ensures from the original precondition to the invariant at 0 at pctop) /\
        (!i. <guards: i < t, ~(i = t), t nonzero/positive>
             ==> ensures from the invariant at i at pctop
                         to the invariant at (i+1) at pcbot,
                         with the invariant's trailing flag component attached) /\
        (!i. <guards: i < t, ~(i = t), t nonzero/positive, i positive, ~(i = 0)>
             ==> ensures from pcbot back to pctop at the same index i) /\
        (ensures from the invariant at t at pcbot to the original postcondition)
}
\noindent In each intermediate pre/postcondition the program-loading condition
and the current program counter are inserted, and the original frame
({MAYCHANGE ...}) is reused unchanged in every conjunct.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show exactly this single-goal transformation, on goals
whose conclusion is an {ensures} for a machine model.

\ENDDOC
